---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/development-piaget-to-today">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Psychology lesson, and it builds on the foundational lesson on
    memory: there you saw the experimental method applied to the mind, and here the same
    methods turn to a bigger question - how a mind gets built in the first place. No prior
    developmental psychology is assumed; the theories, methods and studies are introduced as
    they appear.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain Piaget's mechanism - schemas, assimilation, accommodation - and outline his four stages;</li>
    <li>describe the looking-time method and what it overturned about Piaget's timetable;</li>
    <li>define the zone of proximal development and scaffolding, and say why they changed teaching;</li>
    <li>classify the attachment patterns and state what longitudinal studies do and do not claim about them;</li>
    <li>weigh a developmental claim - stages, sensitive periods, the marshmallow test - against modern replication evidence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Developmental psychology studies <strong>how minds get built</strong> — from newborn reflexes
    to adult reasoning — and its history is the story of three great arguments: Piaget against
    Vygotsky about whether children construct knowledge alone or absorb it socially, Piaget
    against the infant labs about how much babies already know, and Bowlby against everyone
    about how much of adult life is set in the first three years. Modern findings braid all of
    it with genetics: infants know far more than anyone guessed, culture matters more than
    Piaget allowed, and early caregiving leaves measurable traces that are strong but not
    destiny.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the theorists, the overturns, and what survived" widget="equationStep" config={devCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order and read them as
    one argument: a theory of construction (Piaget), a rival about where construction happens
    (Vygotsky), a method that overturns the first theory's timetable (the infant labs), and
    the research programme with the longest reach (attachment). Notice that each step refines
    rather than erases its predecessor - that is how this field actually moved.
  </p>

  <h2>Piaget: the child as a scientist with a theory</h2>
  <p>
    Piaget's core claim is about <em>mechanism</em>: children are not passive sponges but
    active theory-builders. Knowledge lives in <strong>schemas</strong> — organised action
    patterns and concepts — which are revised by two operations: <strong>assimilation</strong>
    (new experience fits the old schema — a toddler who has "dog" extends it to a cat) and
    <strong>accommodation</strong> (the schema changes — dog gets split from cat). When
    predictions keep failing, the system seeks <strong>equilibration</strong>: a better theory.
    On top of this engine, Piaget mounted a stage sequence. In the <strong>sensorimotor</strong>
    stage (0-2), knowledge is action: object permanence — the grasp that things persist when
    hidden — emerges around 8 months, after which infants search. The
    <strong>preoperational</strong> stage (2-7) is symbolic but one-dimensional: children fail
    <strong>conservation</strong> (judging the taller glass holds "more" after watching the
    pour), and <strong>egocentrism</strong> shows in the three-mountains task — the child
    cannot describe a scene from another's vantage. <strong>Concrete operations</strong> (7-11)
    bring reversibility and logical manipulation of real objects; <strong>formal
    operations</strong> (11+) bring the hypothetical: Piaget's pendulum task, where the
    reasoner must vary length and weight systematically to isolate the variable, is solved by
    systematic thinkers.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each concept with its mechanism" widget="match" config={devPiagetMatch} />
  <p>
    The stage theory has aged badly in its details; the method changed the field. Children
    fail Piaget's tasks for mixed reasons — language load, memory demands, the oddness of
    repeated questioning — and small redesigns let younger children pass (hide a smaller
    policeman's office and 3-4-year-olds stop searching where the doll can see them). The
    enduring lessons are two: children's <em>thinking</em> differs qualitatively from adults',
    not just in amount; and the way to learn what a child knows is to watch it fail
    systematically.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the faulty line — a Piaget briefing" widget="spot-error" config={devStageSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Piaget's ages are a timetable children must meet. They
  were lower bounds from small samples and demanding tasks — modern methods put most
  competencies years earlier, and stage boundaries blur into continuous, uneven growth
  (children can be formal thinkers about football and concrete about chemistry).</p>

  <h2>The infant lab: looking time as a window</h2>
  <p>
    The overturn came from a method trick: babies look longer at events that surprise them.
    Baillargeon's <strong>drawbridge experiment</strong> (1987): an infant watches a hinged
    panel swing up, a box is placed behind it, and the panel either stops at the box
    (possible) or swings through where the box must be (impossible). At 4-5 months, babies
    look reliably longer at the impossible event — they expected the box to still exist. The
    arithmetic version is Wynn's (1992): a 5-month-old watches 1 + 1 Mickey Mouse dolls go
    behind a screen; when the screen drops to reveal one doll, looking time spikes. The
    social version is Hamlin, Wynn and Bloom (2007): 6- and 10-month-olds, given the choice,
    reach for a puppet that helped a climber over one that hindered it — a preference for
    helpers with almost no social experience. Together these created "core knowledge":
    expectations about objects, number (approximately), and social value present before
    language.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="scrub the dated studies of the lesson" widget="timeline" config={devStudiesTimeline} />
  <p>
    Hold the enthusiasm at the right voltage: looking-time effects are inferences from
    differential looking, they require careful controls, and several infant-cognition
    findings have weakened in large-scale replications — the field now demands pre-registered,
    multi-lab designs (the ManyBabies project exists precisely to audit this). The safest
    summary: infants are far more competent than Piaget's search tasks implied, but "moral
    baby" claims run ahead of the evidence.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="bet on the safest summary" widget="predict" config={devReplicationPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> Piaget's mechanism - children actively building and revising
    knowledge - has survived; his timetable has not. The next theorist keeps the active
    child but insists the construction is social first.
  </p>

  <h2>Vygotsky: the child as an apprentice</h2>
  <p>
    Vygotsky's counter-thesis: higher thinking is <strong>internalised social
    interaction</strong>. Every mental function appears twice — first between people, then
    inside the child — and the tool it travels through is <strong>language</strong>. His
    signature concept is the <strong>zone of proximal development</strong> (ZPD): the gap
    between what a child can do alone and what she can do with help. Testing with assistance,
    Vygotsky argued, is more predictive than testing alone, because it measures the growing
    edge rather than the frozen past. Bruner operationalised this as <strong>scaffolding</strong>:
    the adult supports, models, and gradually withdraws — tutoring studies showed mothers
    naturally calibrating help to the child's success rate. Private speech (children
    narrating their own actions) is Vygotskyan self-talk — the social dialogue going
    underground and becoming planning; it increases on hard tasks, the opposite of what a
    pure-maturation view would expect. Educationally, the ZPD is why mixed-ability
    grouping, tutoring and worked examples work: the mechanism is assistance pitched just
    beyond independent reach.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="order the scaffolding arc" widget="order" config={devZpdOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two theories of how thinking grows - alone through action, or
    internalised from social interaction. The next section adds the question both largely
    left open: what the earliest relationships do to the mind that is growing.
  </p>

  <h2>Attachment: the first relationship, and its long reach</h2>
  <p>
    Bowlby's synthesis (ethology + psychoanalysis): human infants are born to seek
    proximity to a protective adult because, across evolution, that behaviour kept them
    alive; from thousands of interactions the infant builds an <strong>internal working
    model</strong> of self-worth and other-reliability. Ainsworth made it measurable with
    the <strong>Strange Situation</strong> (1970s): a 12-18-month-old, mother, a stranger,
    and two separations, coded on reunion behaviour. In typical US middle-class samples,
    about 65% are <strong>secure</strong> (distressed at separation, comforted on return,
    then back to play), roughly 20% <strong>insecure-avoidant</strong> (minimal outward
    distress or reunion response — though physiological arousal shows they are not calm),
    and 10-15% <strong>insecure-resistant</strong> (intense distress, hard to settle,
    clingy yet angry). Harlow's monkeys (1958) supplied the mechanism's edge: infants
    raised with wire "mothers" that fed them and cloth ones that did not spent their time
    on the cloth — <strong>contact comfort</strong>, not food, attaches.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag the divider — wire versus cloth" widget="compare" config={devHarlowCompare} />
  <p>
    The long reach is real but bounded. Sroufe's Minnesota longitudinal study followed
    children for decades: infant attachment predicted later competence, and insecure
    attachment raised risks — with continuity far from absolute, and sensitive caregiving
    able to redirect trajectories (the "earned secure" pattern). The tragic natural
    experiment is the Romanian orphanages: children raised with minimal responsive caregiving
    showed lasting cognitive and social deficits, and the Bucharest Early Intervention
    Project (a randomised trial of foster placement) found recovery strongly constrained by
    age — placed before about two, children recovered much more. Sensitive periods exist;
    the dose and timing matter.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> attachment styles are fixed personality types set in
  infancy. They are relationship patterns — decently stable but responsive to experience,
  quality of later caregiving, and therapeutic relationships; and the classification's
  base rates differ across cultures (more "avoidant" classifications appear in some
  European samples, likely reflecting different child-rearing norms rather than
  pathology).</p>

  <h2>Theory of mind: from hiding games to the eyes test</h2>
  <p>
    Around age four comes a conceptual leap: understanding that <em>other people act on
    their beliefs, not on reality</em> — the <strong>false-belief task</strong>. In the
    Sally-Anne paradigm (Baron-Cohen, Leslie & Frith, 1985) a doll hides a marble and
    leaves; another moves it; children are asked where Sally will look. Most 4-year-olds
    say "where Sally left it"; most 3-year-olds say where it really is, unable to hold
    another's mistaken view. This is a cognitive approach result in full: behaviour
    (answering) revealing an internal model (belief representation). The same research
    programme scaled up: Baron-Cohen's later <strong>Reading the Mind in the Eyes
    Test</strong> found adults on the autism spectrum, on average, extract less mental-state
    information from the eye region — developmental research feeding directly into
    clinical measurement. And the developmental lesson runs the other way too: Pozzulo's
    line-up work shows child witnesses more prone than adults to false identification when
    the culprit is absent — a developmental difference with courtroom consequences, and a
    reminder that "how thinking changes" includes how testimony changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Language: the hourglass of development</h2>
  <p>
    Language acquisition deserves its own ledger because its timetable is the
    developmental evidence in miniature. The milestones run on a clock nearly
    independent of instruction: cooing at 2-4 months, <strong>babbling</strong> at
    around 6 months (deaf babies exposed to sign "babble" manually — the syllable
    engine is modality-general), first words near 12 months, a vocabulary burst and
    telegraphic two-word speech in the second year, then grammar. The diagnostic
    gem is <strong>overregularisation</strong>: children say "goed" and "foots" —
    errors their parents never model — proving they are extracting rules, not
    imitating: the child is a hypothesis-tester (Piaget would nod) acquiring a
    cultural tool nobody hands over complete (Vygotsky would nod). The
    <strong>sensitive period</strong> evidence is the strongest in psychology:
    Johnson and Newport's study of Chinese and Korean immigrants to the US found
    grammar-test scores declining steeply with age of arrival, plateauing only
    for those who arrived before roughly 7-10; late first-language learners of
    sign language never fully acquire the grammar they missed; and the tragic
    deprivation cases (Genie and similar) converge on the same ceiling. The
    window is a rate-limiter, not an on/off switch — but it is real, measurable,
    and the reason early language exposure (including bilingual exposure, which
    harms nothing and enriches metalinguistic insight) is one of the few
    development claims with policy-grade certainty.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> growing up with two languages delays or confuses language
  development. The milestone clock runs the same for bilingual children, and the sensitive-period
  evidence points the same way: early exposure is what matters, more of it harms nothing, and
  bilingual exposure enriches metalinguistic insight - awareness of how language itself works.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — the bilingual myth" widget="judge" config={devBilingualJudge} />

  <h2>Adolescence: the second construction site</h2>
  <p>
    The prefrontal cortex — planning, impulse control, weighing consequences — keeps
    remodelling into the mid-20s, while reward circuitry matures earlier. That asymmetry
    is the modern reading of teenage risk-taking: a strong accelerator with brakes that
    are still being installed, especially when peers are watching (adolescents take far
    more risks in company than alone in driving-simulator studies). Executive function
    (working memory, inhibition, flexibility) predicts school outcomes broadly; Mischel's
    marshmallow studies made delay of gratification famous — preschoolers who waited
    longer did better academically and socially years later — but the 2018 reanalysis
    (Watts, Duncan & Quan) with a larger, more diverse sample cut the effect roughly in
    half, most of it explained by family background. Self-control matters; the original
    studies overstated how much it acts alone, and claims that preschool delay "determines"
    life outcomes do not survive the modern data.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="call the remodelling horizon" widget="estimate" config={devPfcEstimate} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A child insists a tall thin glass holds more water than a short wide one, even after watching the pour. Which stage is this, and which skill is missing?</li>
    <li>Baillargeon's drawbridge study used looking time rather than searching. What could infants show with the new method that the old one hid?</li>
    <li>In the Strange Situation, an infant explores freely and barely reacts to separation or reunion. Why is "independently attached" the wrong description of this pattern?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Preoperational, and the missing skill is conservation -
    knowing quantity is unchanged by appearance; the child focuses on one salient dimension
    and cannot yet reverse the transformation mentally. (2) The new method measured
    expectations rather than motor competence: infants looked longer at impossible events,
    revealing object permanence months before they could search for hidden objects - Piaget's
    searching tasks had been measuring the wrong thing. (3) The pattern is insecure-avoidant:
    the infant minimises outward signs of attachment, but physiological measures show arousal -
    the classification is read from reunion behaviour, and avoidant does not mean unattached.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Children are active theory-builders: schemas revised through assimilation and accommodation is Piaget's durable mechanism.</li>
    <li>The stage timetable aged badly; the method - watching children fail systematically - changed the field.</li>
    <li>Looking-time studies show infants know far more than search tasks implied, but infant findings demand large-scale replication.</li>
    <li>Vygotsky: higher thinking is internalised social interaction; the ZPD measures the growing edge, and scaffolding is help pitched just beyond independent reach.</li>
    <li>Attachment patterns are relationship patterns: stable but not destiny, and responsive to later caregiving.</li>
    <li>Sensitive periods are real and measurable - language is the strongest case - but they are rate-limiters, not on/off switches.</li>
    <li>Modern developmental claims must survive pre-registered replication; the 2018 marshmallow reanalysis is the model.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate Psychology shelf continues from here: the research-methods lesson shows
    how the evidence in this lesson is actually produced and audited, and the social-psychology
    lesson takes the mind this chapter watched being built and puts it among other minds.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Nature and nurture braided, not weighed.</strong> The field has abandoned the
    contest for interactionism: genes shape how children evoke and select environments
    (a fearless toddler seeks climbs; the environment shapes what climbing builds), and
    sensitive periods make experience biologically consequential — DeCasper and Fifer
    showed newborns preferring their mother's voice heard in utero; late-exposed deaf
    children of hearing parents never gain native-like grammar of sign, while native
    signers' language systems develop normally (the strongest sensitive-period evidence
    outside birdsong). Development is construction all the way down — by a builder with
    innate tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Which infant social preferences survive rigorous
    replication? How much of the attachment-behaviour link in humans is causal rather
    than genetic confounding (twin and intervention studies split the difference)? Is
    formal operational thinking stage-like, domain-bound, or a cultural product of
    schooling (cross-cultural work suggests the last, in part)? And can brain-based
    "school readiness" measures beat a teacher's judgement? The founding argument — how
    much is built in, how much built — is now an engineering question about
    mechanism, which is exactly what its founders would have wanted.
  </p>
